    {
      "filename": "Fazeley Studios, 191 Fazeley Street, Digbeth (5029494634).jpg",
      "title": "File:Fazeley Studios, 191 Fazeley Street, Digbeth (5029494634).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/Fazeley_Studios%2C_191_Fazeley_Street%2C_Digbeth_%285029494634%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fazeley_Studios,_191_Fazeley_Street,_Digbeth_(5029494634).jpg",
      "width": 3648,
      "height": 2736,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "Elliott Brown from Birmingham, United Kingdom",
      "credit": "Fazeley Studios, 191 Fazeley Street, Digbeth - former Unitarian Sunday School",
      "source": "Fazeley Studios, 191 Fazeley Street, Digbeth (5029494634)",
      "description": "At the end of Fazeley Street (at the junction with Floodgate Street) is Fazeley Studios.\nIt is at 191 Fazeley Street.\nIt is the home of Ikon Eastside.\n\nThe building on the corner with Floodgate Street was the Unitarian Sunday School of 1865 by A B Phipson, extended to the street corner in 1868.\nHistory\nFazeley Studios might be a 21st century creative community, but architecturally, the building dates back nearly 150 years to when Digbeth was a gritty industrial and commercial centre. We've done all we can to preserve this heritage, maintaining a strong sense of continuity with the past.\nThe grand central reception was formerly a Unitarian Chapel designed by George Ingall and opened in 1876.\nThe oldest part of the building was constructed as a Unitarian Sunday school in 1865, and now houses a spacious conference room and a group of character studios.\nThe north light studios occupy a series of factory buildings dating from the 1920s.\nDigbeth was first settled in the 6th century and became an important centre for industry in medieval times as local rivers were harnessed to power its mills, before playing its part in the industrial revolution. In the 21st century, the area's excellent accessibility, facilities and affordability are promoting it as the Creative and Digital capital of the region.\n\nFazeley Studios was conceived in 2006, and with the support of Advantage West Midlands has been the subject of a comprehensive reconstruction.",
      "dateCreated": "2010-09-26 11:08",
      "categories": [
        "CC-BY-2.0",
        "Fazeley Street Chapel and Sunday School, Digbeth",
        "Files with coordinates missing SDC location of creation (52° N, 2° W)",
        "Flickr images reviewed by FlickreviewR"
      ],
      "sourceType": "core",
      "sourceArticleId": "ikon-gallery-wp",
